{"meta": {"course": "inst-design", "chapter": "02", "section": "03", "chapter_title": "The ADDIE Framework", "title": "Module 3: The Evaluation Phase", "description": "Use formative and summative evidence to judge whether instruction is working, and interpret Kirkpatrick's model as a layered evaluation framework.", "learning_objectives": ["Distinguish between Formative and Summative evaluation.", "Apply Kirkpatrick's four levels of evaluation.", "Design evidence-collection plans that align evaluation questions with instructional goals."], "concept_ids": ["evaluation_phase", "formative_evaluation", "summative_evaluation", "kirkpatrick_model", "evidence_collection"], "prereq_section_ids": ["inst-design/02/02"], "practice_ids": ["formative_vs_summative", "kirkpatrick_learning", "reaction_not_learning", "evaluation_evidence_choice"], "estimated_time_minutes": 10}, "content": "# Evaluating Impact and Psychometrics\n\n> **\"Not everything that can be counted counts, and not everything that counts can be counted.\"**  \n> - attributed to William Bruce Cameron\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Distinguish** formative from summative evaluation and place each correctly in a design timeline\n2. **Map** an evaluation question to the appropriate Kirkpatrick level of evidence\n3. **Calculate** program ROI and adjust it for the isolation problem using a confidence factor\n4. **Interpret** a 2-parameter IRT model to judge the quality of an assessment item, not just the learner\n5. **Diagnose** where an evaluation plan is likely to overclaim or collect the wrong evidence\n\n---\n\n## Opening: The Course That Everybody Loved\n\nA regional hospital rolled out a four-hour patient-safety course. The end-of-session surveys were glowing: 4.8 out of 5, comments praising the facilitator, near-perfect post-test scores. The training team celebrated and the budget was renewed.\n\nSix months later, the infection-control office pulled hand-hygiene compliance logs from the wards. Compliance had not moved. The course that everybody loved had changed almost no behavior on the floor.\n\nNothing in that story is unusual. It is the single most common failure in instructional evaluation: collecting evidence that is easy to gather (smile sheets, quiz scores) while quietly assuming it stands in for evidence that is hard to gather (real behavior change, organizational results). Evaluation, done well, is the discipline that keeps a design team honest about the difference.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Evaluation is the empirical backbone of instructional design. It is the only way to show stakeholders that a training intervention actually closed the performance gap identified in analysis and justified the cost of design, development, delivery, and maintenance.\n\nEvaluation is traditionally viewed as the \"final\" phase of the ADDIE model. However, strong instructional designers understand that evaluation must be continuous. If you only measure at the very end, you have no data to correct course during development, and no baseline against which to claim a change later.\n\n### A Designer's First Reframe\n\nEvaluation is not only about asking whether learners liked the course. It is about deciding:\n\n- what counts as evidence,\n- when evidence should be collected,\n- how performance should be interpreted,\n- and whether observed change can reasonably be attributed to the instruction.\n\nThat last point, attribution, is what separates a persuasive story from a defensible claim. That is why evaluation belongs in design conversations early, not just in end-of-course reporting.\n\n---\n\n## Formative vs. Summative Evaluation\n\n- **Formative evaluation:** happens during design and development. It helps teams improve the instruction before full rollout.\n- **Summative evaluation:** happens after implementation. It helps teams judge whether the instruction produced meaningful results.\n\nA useful mnemonic from Robert Stake: when the cook tastes the soup, that is formative; when the guests taste the soup, that is summative.\n\n| Dimension | Formative | Summative |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Timing | During design and development | After implementation |\n| Purpose | Improve the instruction | Judge the instruction |\n| Audience | Design team, SMEs | Stakeholders, sponsors |\n| Typical question | \"What is confusing or broken?\" | \"Did it work, and was it worth it?\" |\n| Risk if skipped | Costly defects ship to learners | No evidence of value or impact |\n\nTypical formative methods include usability tests, pilot runs, SME reviews, cognitive walkthroughs, and think-aloud sessions with representative learners.\n\nTypical summative methods include pre and post assessments, transfer checks on the job, learner surveys, manager observations, and business-performance indicators.\n\n<formative-summative-diagram></formative-summative-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## The Kirkpatrick Model and What Each Level Really Means\n\nKirkpatrick's Four Levels remain one of the most influential frameworks in instructional design because they help separate weak evidence from stronger evidence.\n\n### Level 1: Reaction\n\nThis level asks whether learners found the experience engaging, relevant, and usable.\n\n- useful signals: satisfaction, relevance, confidence, usability feedback\n- common tools: surveys, ratings, comments, short reflections\n- limitation: high enjoyment does not prove learning\n\n### Level 2: Learning\n\nThis level asks whether learners actually acquired targeted knowledge, skills, or attitudes.\n\n- useful signals: pre/post gains, scored demonstrations, rubric performance\n- common tools: tests, scenarios, oral defenses, simulations\n- limitation: strong performance in training does not guarantee transfer\n\n### Level 3: Behavior\n\nThis level asks whether learners apply what they learned in real performance settings.\n\n- useful signals: observed transfer, workflow changes, performance logs\n- common tools: manager observation, peer review, delayed tasks, field audits\n- limitation: workplace conditions often interfere with transfer\n\n### Level 4: Results\n\nThis level asks whether behavior change affected meaningful organizational outcomes.\n\n- useful signals: quality gains, fewer incidents, faster completion, reduced cost\n- common tools: KPI dashboards, operational metrics, longitudinal comparison\n- limitation: many outside variables may influence the result\n\n<inline-check question=\"A sales team posts excellent end-of-workshop satisfaction scores AND high post-test scores, but quarterly call-quality audits three months later show no change in how reps actually run calls. Which Kirkpatrick level is the failure?\" explanation=\"Reaction (Level 1, satisfaction) and Learning (Level 2, post-test) both succeeded. The breakdown is at Behavior (Level 3): the learned skill did not transfer to real performance on the job. Results (Level 4) cannot even be assessed until behavior changes.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Level 3, Behavior: the skill did not transfer to the job\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Level 1, Reaction: the workshop was disliked\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Level 2, Learning: nothing was learned\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThis progression helps designers avoid overclaiming. A strong Level 1 result does not substitute for Level 3 evidence. A strong Level 2 result does not automatically imply Level 4 impact. Notice that effort and cost rise as you climb the levels, while the directness of business value also rises. Most organizations stop at Level 1 and 2 precisely because they are cheap, which is exactly why so many programs are renewed without proof that they changed anything that matters.\n\n---\n\n## A More Practical Selection Guide\n\nWhen choosing evaluation methods, start with the decision the organization needs to make, not with the instrument you happen to have.\n\n| If the team needs to... | Prioritize | Primary evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Improve the course before launch | Formative methods | Usability, think-alouds, SME review |\n| Prove learning happened | Level 2 | Pre/post gains, scored demonstrations |\n| Justify rollout or renewal | Levels 3 and 4 | Transfer observation, KPI shifts |\n| Compare two versions | Baseline + comparison window | Controlled A/B with matched groups |\n\nThis framing helps the designer choose evidence that is decision-relevant rather than merely easy to collect. The guiding rule: the stronger the consequence of the decision, the stronger the evidence should be.\n\n---\n\n## Phillips ROI and the Isolation Problem\n\nJack Phillips added a fifth layer focused on return on investment. The logic is appealing: translate impact into money, compare it to program cost, and calculate ROI.\n\n$$ ROI (\\%) = \\frac{\\text{Net Program Benefits}}{\\text{Program Costs}} \\times 100 $$\n\nwhere net program benefits equal monetized benefits minus program costs.\n\nBut this raises the central methodological problem in evaluation: **isolation**. If sales increase after sales training, what caused the increase: the training, a new marketing campaign, a seasonal trend, a change in incentives, or a weaker competitor?\n\nPhillips' practical answer is the **isolation factor**: ask credible sources (managers, participants, analysts) to estimate what fraction of the improvement the training is responsible for, together with how confident they are. The raw benefit is then discounted before any ROI is reported.\n\n$$ \\text{Adjusted Benefits} = \\text{Raw Benefits} \\times (\\text{Attribution \\%}) \\times (\\text{Confidence \\%}) $$\n\nDrag the attribution and confidence factors and watch the defensible ROI separate from the naive headline. Notice how fast a plausible-sounding 200% headline collapses once you discount for what the training truly caused.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Isolation-adjusted ROI explorer\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"raw\",\"label\":\"Raw monetized benefit\",\"min\":50000,\"max\":300000,\"step\":5000,\"unit\":\"$\",\"default\":180000},{\"name\":\"cost\",\"label\":\"Program cost\",\"min\":20000,\"max\":120000,\"step\":5000,\"unit\":\"$\",\"default\":60000},{\"name\":\"attr\",\"label\":\"Attribution to training\",\"min\":0,\"max\":100,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":70},{\"name\":\"conf\",\"label\":\"Source confidence\",\"min\":0,\"max\":100,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":100}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Naive ROI\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"(raw - cost) / cost * 100\"},{\"label\":\"Adjusted ROI\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"(raw * (attr/100) * (conf/100) - cost) / cost * 100\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"attr\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n### Worked Example: ROI With and Without Isolation\n\n**Problem.** A leadership program costs **\\$60,000** to design and deliver. After rollout, the business attributes **\\$180,000** in monetized improvement (faster project delivery, lower turnover) to the cohort. Managers estimate the program is responsible for about **70%** of that gain. Compute the naive ROI and the isolation-adjusted ROI.\n\n**Step 1: Naive net benefit.**\n\n$$ \\text{Net} = \\$180{,}000 - \\$60{,}000 = \\$120{,}000 $$\n\n**Step 2: Naive ROI.**\n\n$$ ROI = \\frac{120{,}000}{60{,}000} \\times 100 = \\boxed{200\\%} $$\n\nThis is the number an unrigorous report would headline: every dollar returned three.\n\n**Step 3: Apply the isolation factor.** Only 70% of the \\$180,000 is credibly the program's doing.\n\n$$ \\text{Adjusted Benefits} = \\$180{,}000 \\times 0.70 = \\$126{,}000 $$\n\n$$ \\text{Adjusted Net} = \\$126{,}000 - \\$60{,}000 = \\$66{,}000 $$\n\n**Step 4: Adjusted ROI.**\n\n$$ ROI_{adj} = \\frac{66{,}000}{60{,}000} \\times 100 = \\boxed{110\\%} $$\n\nThe program is still a good investment, but the defensible figure (110%) is nearly half the headline figure (200%). Reporting the 200% would not be a lie so much as a failure of attribution, and the first sharp CFO will dismantle it. Without control groups, baselines, delayed follow-up, triangulation across sources, and explicit acknowledgment of confounders, ROI claims sound persuasive but remain analytically weak.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Advanced Evaluation\" context=\"A data scientist is trying to prove to the CFO that a new leadership training program produced measurable ROI while controlling for staffing changes and broader market shifts.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Advanced Evaluation: Item Response Theory\n\nIn digital assessment systems, percent-correct scoring is often not enough. Two learners can both score 7 out of 10 yet have very different ability if one got the hard items right and the other got the easy items right. **Item Response Theory (IRT)** improves measurement by modeling both learners and items on the same latent scale.\n\nIn a 2-parameter logistic (2PL) model, each question is described by:\n\n- **Difficulty (b):** how much latent ability a learner needs to have a 50 percent chance of success\n- **Discrimination (a):** how sharply the item separates lower-ability and higher-ability learners\n\n$$ P(X_i = 1 \\mid \\theta, a_i, b_i) = \\frac{e^{a_i(\\theta - b_i)}}{1 + e^{a_i(\\theta - b_i)}} $$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is learner ability, $a_i$ is item discrimination, $b_i$ is item difficulty, and $P(X_i = 1)$ is the probability of a correct response.\n\nManipulate the 2PL item curve directly. Set a learner ability, then watch the probability of a correct response as difficulty and discrimination change. Push discrimination toward zero and notice how the probability crowds toward 0.5, which is exactly the signature of an item that cannot tell strong learners from weak ones.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"2PL item characteristic curve\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"theta\",\"label\":\"Learner ability (theta)\",\"min\":-3,\"max\":3,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":1},{\"name\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Discrimination (a)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":2.5,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":1.2},{\"name\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Difficulty (b)\",\"min\":-3,\"max\":3,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.5}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"P(correct)\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"1 / (1 + 2.71828^(0 - a*(theta - b)))\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"theta\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer> Ability and difficulty are on the same scale, so the quantity that drives the probability is the gap $(\\theta - b_i)$: a learner well above an item's difficulty is likely to pass it; a learner well below is likely to miss it.\n\n### Worked Example: Reading an Item With IRT\n\n**Problem.** An item has discrimination $a = 1.2$ and difficulty $b = 0.5$. Find the probability of a correct response for a strong learner at $\\theta = 1.0$ and a weaker learner at $\\theta = 0.0$.\n\n**Step 1: Strong learner, $\\theta = 1.0$.** Compute the exponent.\n\n$$ a(\\theta - b) = 1.2 \\,(1.0 - 0.5) = 0.6 $$\n\n**Step 2: Apply the logistic function.** Using $e^{0.6} \\approx 1.822$,\n\n$$ P = \\frac{1.822}{1 + 1.822} = \\frac{1.822}{2.822} = \\boxed{0.646} $$\n\n**Step 3: Weaker learner, $\\theta = 0.0$.**\n\n$$ a(\\theta - b) = 1.2\\,(0.0 - 0.5) = -0.6, \\qquad e^{-0.6} \\approx 0.549 $$\n\n$$ P = \\frac{0.549}{1 + 0.549} = \\boxed{0.354} $$\n\nStep through the same computation yourself before reading the interpretation.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Reading the 2PL item by hand\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"For the strong learner, theta = 1.0, with a = 1.2 and b = 0.5, compute the exponent a(theta - b).\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"0.6\",\"1.2\",\"-0.6\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"a(theta - b) = 1.2 * (1.0 - 0.5) = 0.6. The learner sits above the item difficulty, so the exponent is positive.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Apply the logistic function with e^0.6 = 1.822. Will P be above or below 0.5?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Above 0.5\",\"Below 0.5\",\"Exactly 0.5\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"P = 1.822 / (1 + 1.822) = 0.646. A positive exponent always yields P above 0.5.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Now the weaker learner at theta = 0.0. Predict the sign of the exponent.\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Negative\",\"Positive\",\"Zero\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"a(theta - b) = 1.2 * (0.0 - 0.5) = -0.6, so e^(-0.6) = 0.549 and P = 0.549 / 1.549 = 0.354.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The spread (0.646 vs 0.354) is what a healthy discrimination buys. If a were 0.2, both probabilities would crowd toward 0.5 and the item would barely separate ability levels: a signal to revise or retire it.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n**Interpretation.** The same item gives a 64.6% chance of success to the stronger learner and only a 35.4% chance to the weaker one. That spread is what a healthy discrimination value buys you: the item meaningfully separates ability levels. If you reran this with a low discrimination such as $a = 0.2$, the two probabilities would crowd toward 0.5 and the item would barely tell strong and weak learners apart, which is a signal to revise or retire it.\n\nIRT helps designers do more than score learners. It helps them inspect the **quality of the assessment itself**. A poorly written item may confuse experts, reward guessing, or depend on language complexity unrelated to the intended construct. If designers never examine item quality, they may misdiagnose learner performance when the actual problem lies in the assessment instrument.\n\n---\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Why it happens | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Treating Level 1 as proof of impact | Smile sheets are cheap and flattering | Pre-commit to at least one Level 3 measure |\n| Headline ROI with no isolation | The big number is persuasive | Apply attribution and confidence factors |\n| Measuring only at the end | Evaluation framed as the last ADDIE phase | Build formative checkpoints into development |\n| No baseline | Nobody captured \"before\" | Define baseline and comparison window in design |\n| Scoring learners but never items | Percent-correct feels objective | Use IRT or item analysis to audit the instrument |\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"evaluation_phase\",\"formative_evaluation\",\"summative_evaluation\",\"kirkpatrick_model\",\"evidence_collection\"]'></concept-map>\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Program ROI | $ROI(\\%) = \\dfrac{\\text{Net Benefits}}{\\text{Costs}} \\times 100$ |\n| Adjusted benefits | $\\text{Raw} \\times \\text{Attribution} \\times \\text{Confidence}$ |\n| 2PL item model | $P = \\dfrac{e^{a(\\theta - b)}}{1 + e^{a(\\theta - b)}}$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Evaluation is continuous, not a final step; formative work prevents costly defects and creates the baseline summative work depends on.\n- Match the evidence to the decision; climb Kirkpatrick's levels as the stakes rise.\n- A glowing Level 1 result tells you nothing reliable about Level 3 behavior or Level 4 results.\n- ROI is only as honest as its isolation; always discount raw benefits for attribution and confidence.\n- IRT evaluates the test as well as the test-taker; an item that fails to discriminate is an item to fix.\n\n---\n\n## A Checklist for Better Evaluation Design\n\nBefore finalizing an evaluation plan, ask:\n\n- What decision will this evidence support?\n- Which level of evidence is actually needed?\n- What baseline exists for comparison?\n- How will transfer or retention be checked later?\n- What alternate explanations could distort the conclusion?\n\nThe stronger the consequence of the decision, the stronger the evidence should be.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nWith evaluation in place, the design loop is complete: analysis defines the gap, design and development build the solution, implementation delivers it, and evaluation tells you whether any of it worked. The next module turns to **rapid prototyping and iteration**, where formative evaluation stops being an end-of-phase audit and becomes the engine that drives each design cycle.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A hospital's hand-washing course receives excellent satisfaction ratings and surgeons score very high on the post-test, but hidden observation later shows that hand-washing behavior on the job has not improved. What is the clearest diagnosis?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"The training succeeded fully because Levels 1 and 2 were strong.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"The course mainly failed at Level 3 because knowledge did not transfer reliably into workplace behavior.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"The problem is only with Level 4 because no financial ROI was calculated.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. The evidence shows that learners reacted positively and performed well in training, but the desired behavior did not transfer into the real setting.\"></interactive-quiz>\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "formative_vs_summative", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "formative_evaluation", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["formative_evaluation", "summative_evaluation"], "misconception_id": "evaluation_only_at_end", "stem": "What is the main purpose of formative evaluation?", "options": ["To judge final impact only after the course is complete", "To catch design problems during development so revisions can still be made", "To replace learner testing with SME opinion only", "To measure ROI after six months of implementation"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Formative evaluation happens during design and development so teams can improve the instruction before launch."}, {"id": "kirkpatrick_learning", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "kirkpatrick_model", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["kirkpatrick_model"], "misconception_id": "kirkpatrick_levels_mixed", "stem": "Which Kirkpatrick level asks whether learners actually acquired knowledge or skill?", "options": ["Level 1: Reaction", "Level 2: Learning", "Level 3: Behavior", "Level 4: Results"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Level 2 focuses on whether learning occurred, typically through tests, demonstrations, or performance evidence."}, {"id": "reaction_not_learning", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evaluation_phase", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["evaluation_phase", "kirkpatrick_model"], "misconception_id": "reaction_equals_effectiveness", "stem": "Why is a high post-course satisfaction score not enough to prove instructional effectiveness?", "options": ["Because reaction data can be positive even when real learning or transfer is weak", "Because satisfaction surveys are illegal in most settings", "Because reaction data always measures ROI directly", "Because only psychometricians can collect learner feedback"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "Learners may enjoy a course without learning much. Reaction data is useful, but it does not prove performance improvement by itself."}, {"id": "evaluation_evidence_choice", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_collection", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_collection", "kirkpatrick_model"], "stem": "Which evidence source best aligns with Level 3 behavior evaluation?", "options": ["A smile-sheet rating gathered immediately after class", "A pre/post knowledge quiz taken in the LMS", "Observation of whether employees changed their on-the-job behavior after training", "A brand-style review of the course visuals"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Level 3 asks whether the learner changed behavior in practice, not just whether they liked the course or passed a quiz."}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "32f420bcf418c423d5d738c24a60330a0d22d60db308d275f1ed84ffbce757c2", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "reaction_equals_effectiveness", "pattern": "evidence_error", "description": "Assuming positive learner reactions prove instructional success", "trigger": "learner treats high satisfaction scores as sufficient evidence of impact", "feedback": "Reaction data is useful, but it is only one level of evidence. Strong evaluation also examines learning, behavior change, and organizational results.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "evaluation_only_at_end", "pattern": "process_error", "description": "Treating evaluation as something that happens only after launch", "trigger": "learner ignores formative evaluation during design and development", "feedback": "Evaluation should be continuous. Formative evaluation helps catch problems early, while summative evaluation judges final effectiveness later.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "kirkpatrick_levels_mixed", "pattern": "classification_error", "description": "Confusing reaction, learning, behavior, and results data", "trigger": "learner labels quizzes as Level 1 or on-the-job performance as Level 2", "feedback": "The Kirkpatrick levels answer different questions. Reaction measures impressions, learning measures acquisition, behavior measures transfer, and results measures organizational outcomes.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}